1
00:00:00,192 --> 00:00:04,285
The next step is to create a new non-root

2
00:00:04,509 --> 00:00:06,660
user. Again, the reason here is

3
00:00:07,318 --> 00:00:09,212
that you could of course always use your

4
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:12,598
root user, but if anybody ever gets access

5
00:00:12,775 --> 00:00:16,129
to that VPS through that connection, so if

6
00:00:16,145 --> 00:00:18,408
somebody gets access to your MacBook, for

7
00:00:18,408 --> 00:00:20,912
example, or your Windows machine where

8
00:00:21,040 --> 00:00:23,785
you're storing your private key and they

9
00:00:23,785 --> 00:00:26,193
then connect to that remote server, they

10
00:00:26,337 --> 00:00:28,682
would be connected as a root user and they

11
00:00:28,682 --> 00:00:31,075
could wreak havoc there because they would

12
00:00:31,075 --> 00:00:32,971
have full access to everything. That is why

13
00:00:33,003 --> 00:00:36,488
you typically wanna create a new non-root

14
00:00:36,777 --> 00:00:37,098
user.

15
00:00:37,677 --> 00:00:40,937
You still want to give that user a way of

16
00:00:41,290 --> 00:00:44,422
performing root tasks on that remote machine

17
00:00:44,422 --> 00:00:46,543
though, for example, if it needs to install

18
00:00:47,169 --> 00:00:50,237
software that needs root access like Docker,

19
00:00:50,686 --> 00:00:51,827
but we'll get back to that.

20
00:00:52,870 --> 00:00:55,230
So in order to create a new user, there is

21
00:00:55,262 --> 00:00:57,509
the adduser command, which does what the

22
00:00:57,509 --> 00:00:58,264
name suggests.

23
00:00:58,874 --> 00:01:00,880
And then you can give that user any name of

24
00:01:00,880 --> 00:01:04,364
your choice, admin or Max, because my name

25
00:01:04,412 --> 00:01:06,836
is Max, I'll go with Max here.

26
00:01:07,863 --> 00:01:10,158
Now that adds a new user and you're then

27
00:01:10,415 --> 00:01:11,924
asked to choose a password.

28
00:01:12,823 --> 00:01:14,990
You don't have to choose one, but you might

29
00:01:14,990 --> 00:01:16,403
wanna do that to make sure

30
00:01:17,013 --> 00:01:20,485
that again, if somebody gets access, to that

31
00:01:20,614 --> 00:01:23,215
user or to that connection which will use

32
00:01:23,215 --> 00:01:24,378
that user in the future,

33
00:01:25,073 --> 00:01:27,270
they would still need a password there to do

34
00:01:27,415 --> 00:01:28,707
anything as that user.

35
00:01:29,510 --> 00:01:31,672
So I'll choose a password here

36
00:01:32,673 --> 00:01:34,642
and you could then enter more details like

37
00:01:34,691 --> 00:01:36,853
your name and so on, but I'll leave all of

38
00:01:36,886 --> 00:01:38,970
that blank, confirm that by hitting Enter

39
00:01:38,970 --> 00:01:42,093
again, and with that, a new user was added.

40
00:01:43,629 --> 00:01:44,455
Now, as mentioned,

41
00:01:45,022 --> 00:01:48,312
we want to make sure that this new user can

42
00:01:48,409 --> 00:01:49,690
perform tasks as

43
00:01:50,336 --> 00:01:52,069
a root user though with full

44
00:01:52,823 --> 00:01:55,534
access to everything because when managing

45
00:01:55,647 --> 00:01:57,764
such a remote server, there sometimes are

46
00:01:57,909 --> 00:02:00,123
such tasks. And of course, you could argue

47
00:02:00,123 --> 00:02:02,369
that you can always log in as the root user

48
00:02:02,401 --> 00:02:04,679
if you wanna perform such tasks, but we'll

49
00:02:04,711 --> 00:02:07,888
disable that root user login soon for

50
00:02:07,936 --> 00:02:09,765
security reasons. And therefore, I wanna

51
00:02:09,765 --> 00:02:10,936
give my Max user

52
00:02:11,755 --> 00:02:14,739
optional root access. And to do that in a

53
00:02:14,739 --> 00:02:17,363
secure way, you would run the usermod

54
00:02:17,460 --> 00:02:18,783
command with

55
00:02:21,188 --> 00:02:21,365
-aG

56
00:02:22,495 --> 00:02:25,060
sudo and then your username. So max in my

57
00:02:25,190 --> 00:02:27,130
case, whatever you chose in your case.

58
00:02:28,682 --> 00:02:29,507
Now what does this do?

59
00:02:30,138 --> 00:02:33,485
This modifies my user and adds it to a group

60
00:02:33,615 --> 00:02:35,488
by appending that group.

61
00:02:36,134 --> 00:02:39,698
-a does append. If I had just -G, it would

62
00:02:39,908 --> 00:02:41,989
overwrite the groups that user is part of.

63
00:02:42,270 --> 00:02:43,811
But I don't wanna overwrite the groups. I

64
00:02:43,811 --> 00:02:45,850
just wanna add a new group and that's the

65
00:02:45,850 --> 00:02:46,541
sudo group.

66
00:02:47,199 --> 00:02:49,736
And that's simply a group of users that can

67
00:02:49,848 --> 00:02:50,105
get

68
00:02:50,812 --> 00:02:54,216
sudo access, which is in the end root access

69
00:02:54,489 --> 00:02:56,383
on your VPS here.

70
00:02:57,379 --> 00:02:59,579
So I'll run that and that's all I need to

71
00:02:59,579 --> 00:02:59,787
do.

72
00:03:00,751 --> 00:03:03,513
And with that done, as a next step, I wanna

73
00:03:03,513 --> 00:03:05,584
make sure that this new user which we

74
00:03:05,584 --> 00:03:09,534
created also can connect to that VPS via

75
00:03:09,534 --> 00:03:12,390
that SSH key. So I still have my connection

76
00:03:12,406 --> 00:03:14,380
here with my root user, but I opened a new

77
00:03:14,492 --> 00:03:17,364
terminal window on my Mac. So not on the

78
00:03:17,380 --> 00:03:19,385
remote machine in any way, but instead on my

79
00:03:19,385 --> 00:03:21,021
Mac, I opened a new terminal.

80
00:03:22,032 --> 00:03:24,791
And in there, I now wanna make sure that I

81
00:03:25,144 --> 00:03:29,251
copy the SSH key, which we created earlier

82
00:03:29,251 --> 00:03:31,080
in the course, which is stored on my Mac.

83
00:03:31,754 --> 00:03:33,840
I have the private and the public key stored

84
00:03:33,840 --> 00:03:34,401
on my Mac.

85
00:03:34,946 --> 00:03:36,390
Remember, I have the,

86
00:03:37,833 --> 00:03:41,071
vpscore's private key file and the.pub file

87
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:43,011
with the public key. I have them here. I

88
00:03:43,027 --> 00:03:46,153
wanna copy that public key to that remote

89
00:03:46,153 --> 00:03:49,231
machine for that new user. We didn't have to

90
00:03:49,231 --> 00:03:50,481
do that for the root user

91
00:03:51,283 --> 00:03:53,431
because Hetzner, like many providers,

92
00:03:53,767 --> 00:03:54,954
offered us a way of

93
00:03:55,691 --> 00:03:57,615
providing that key when we set up the

94
00:03:57,615 --> 00:04:00,676
server. And there it then copied that public

95
00:04:00,676 --> 00:04:03,161
key for the root user it was about to set

96
00:04:03,161 --> 00:04:03,305
up.

97
00:04:04,199 --> 00:04:07,032
If we now manually added a new user, the Max

98
00:04:07,032 --> 00:04:09,282
user in my case, I have to manually give it

99
00:04:09,282 --> 00:04:10,026
that public key.

100
00:04:11,010 --> 00:04:14,266
And that is done by using the ssh-copy-id

101
00:04:16,146 --> 00:04:18,219
command on my Mac or on your Windows

102
00:04:18,235 --> 00:04:19,649
machine, wherever you are.

103
00:04:20,661 --> 00:04:20,951
And then

104
00:04:21,690 --> 00:04:21,754
you

105
00:04:22,590 --> 00:04:23,040
choose

106
00:04:23,795 --> 00:04:26,246
the username which you created, Max in my

107
00:04:26,295 --> 00:04:26,538
case,@,

108
00:04:28,079 --> 00:04:30,236
then I need the IP again.

109
00:04:31,968 --> 00:04:34,243
And I want to let it know which key to copy.

110
00:04:35,044 --> 00:04:35,413
So

111
00:04:35,974 --> 00:04:39,211
I add -i and I'll point at my

112
00:04:40,637 --> 00:04:41,967
.pub file like this.

113
00:04:42,624 --> 00:04:44,210
And of course you choose the Windows

114
00:04:44,242 --> 00:04:46,854
equivalent path here with backward slashes

115
00:04:47,351 --> 00:04:49,498
as I showed you earlier in the course if you

116
00:04:49,594 --> 00:04:50,460
are on Windows.

117
00:04:51,117 --> 00:04:54,738
If you hit Enter, this should ask for that

118
00:04:54,802 --> 00:04:57,608
password of that user you created. So I'll

119
00:04:57,688 --> 00:04:58,298
enter that here

120
00:04:59,535 --> 00:05:01,398
and it should then copy that key

121
00:05:01,944 --> 00:05:04,305
onto that machine to that user. And you can

122
00:05:04,305 --> 00:05:06,923
indeed see that key which was just copied by

123
00:05:06,939 --> 00:05:08,946
going to that other terminal where you're

124
00:05:08,946 --> 00:05:11,885
still connected to the VPS through your root

125
00:05:12,078 --> 00:05:12,464
user.

126
00:05:13,443 --> 00:05:16,463
And there you can use the cat command, which

127
00:05:16,559 --> 00:05:19,546
stands for concatenate and which despite its

128
00:05:19,546 --> 00:05:22,100
name is quite handy for simply reading the

129
00:05:22,100 --> 00:05:23,031
contents of a file.

130
00:05:23,866 --> 00:05:24,780
Often used for that.

131
00:05:25,485 --> 00:05:30,342
And you can cat /home/your username, max in

132
00:05:30,374 --> 00:05:30,855
my case,

133
00:05:31,641 --> 00:05:32,106
.ssh,

134
00:05:32,939 --> 00:05:34,125
and then there you'll have an

135
00:05:34,366 --> 00:05:37,524
authorized_keys file. By the way, for this

136
00:05:37,668 --> 00:05:39,816
autocompletion here, you could just type the

137
00:05:39,816 --> 00:05:41,948
starting character and hit Tab and it will

138
00:05:42,141 --> 00:05:42,654
autocomplete.

139
00:05:43,599 --> 00:05:46,148
If you hit Enter, you'll see the contents of

140
00:05:46,148 --> 00:05:47,944
that file there, and that is that public key

141
00:05:48,008 --> 00:05:50,060
which we just copied from our Mac or from

142
00:05:50,092 --> 00:05:51,900
your Windows machine to that VPS.

143
00:05:52,852 --> 00:05:55,628
So that public key is now on the VPS for

144
00:05:55,676 --> 00:05:58,387
that Max user, and therefore this Max user

145
00:05:58,387 --> 00:06:01,209
should now also be able to connect to that

146
00:06:01,307 --> 00:06:04,786
remote server, to that VPS with that SSH

147
00:06:04,818 --> 00:06:06,899
key. And to give that a try, we can simply

148
00:06:06,900 --> 00:06:09,992
try SSH Max instead of root,

149
00:06:10,540 --> 00:06:10,991
Max

150
00:06:11,523 --> 00:06:12,537
or whatever you chose,@

151
00:06:14,164 --> 00:06:15,566
then the IP here,

152
00:06:18,126 --> 00:06:19,852
And then of course, just as before,

153
00:06:21,432 --> 00:06:23,464
point at the key it should use for that.

154
00:06:24,210 --> 00:06:27,475
So in my case,.ssh and then vps-course. And

155
00:06:27,475 --> 00:06:28,239
if I hit Enter,

156
00:06:29,100 --> 00:06:29,912
this now works.

157
00:06:30,682 --> 00:06:32,481
And you can see I'm connected to my

158
00:06:32,530 --> 00:06:35,149
vps-course VPS again, but this time using

159
00:06:35,181 --> 00:06:38,058
the max user. So now both the root and the

160
00:06:38,058 --> 00:06:40,664
max user can connect with that SSH key.

161
00:06:41,277 --> 00:06:43,440
And that completes that second step here

162
00:06:43,633 --> 00:06:45,650
where we create a new non-root user.
